{
  "image_name": "0b4c4ac30378e82362d2ff52c4dda3be.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how three chromosome-organizing complexes—cohesin, condensin, and Smc5/6—support segregation, transcription, and replication by controlling DNA looping and topology.\n\nIn the segregation part, a normal metaphase chromosome is compared with loss of each complex. Without cohesin, sister chromatids lack cohesion and proper biorientation fails. Without condensin, chromosomes appear poorly condensed and tangled, so entanglements persist into anaphase. Without Smc5/6, chromosomes show structural abnormalities and missegregate. The overall message is that each complex contributes differently to building chromosomes that can separate cleanly.\n\nThe transcription part illustrates cohesin forming DNA loops that can either bring an enhancer close to a promoter to activate RNA production or create insulated loops that block enhancer-promoter contact. Condensin, in contrast, is shown compacting chromatin into repeated loops associated with a silenced region, suggesting a role in repression.\n\nThe replication part shows a replisome moving along DNA while Smc5/6 helps manage topological stress ahead of the fork. In its absence, DNA intertwines or supercoiling-like problems accumulate, and replication appears slower. Overall, the figure emphasizes loop extrusion and topology control as central mechanisms linking chromosome structure to genome function.",
